    "q-124034": {
      "dom_id": "step-q124034",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "Let $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $2x=\\pi {y}^{3}$ for $y\\in [0,1],$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.$",
      "worked_solution": "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\nWe start by parametrizing the curve $C.$ A suitable parametrization is\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨\\frac{\\pi {t}^{3}}{2},t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$\n\nThen, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,0⟩, \\\\ r(1) & =⟨\\frac{\\pi }{2},1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(1))-f(r(0)) \\\\  & =f(\\frac{\\pi }{2},1)-f(0,0) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\operatorname{cos}⁡1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡0\\operatorname{cos}⁡0 \\\\  & =0\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡1-1\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
